WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:07.900
Watching a cell shows a part active alongside an outcome.

2
00:00:08.500 --> 00:00:10.560
It cannot show whether the part is needed.

3
00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:12.860
That takes removing the part.

4
00:00:13.680 --> 00:00:19.480
But a sodium current, a receptor or an enzyme cannot be pulled out of a living nerve

5
00:00:19.480 --> 00:00:25.900
by hand. A drug that binds one protein can switch that one part off within minutes,

6
00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:27.700
and often be washed out.

7
00:00:28.550 --> 00:00:32.830
Choose a compound that binds one protein with high affinity.

8
00:00:34.030 --> 00:00:39.570
Tetrodotoxin binds voltage-gated sodium channels, one channel per toxin molecule,

9
00:00:39.910 --> 00:00:42.930
and plugs the pore, so no sodium passes.

10
00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:47.110
Curare binds the muscle acetylcholine receptor.

11
00:00:48.230 --> 00:00:52.070
Add tetrodotoxin to an axon and the action potential is blocked.

12
00:00:52.730 --> 00:00:54.610
The resting potential does not change.

13
00:00:55.483 --> 00:00:58.183
Deliver it where the target is.

14
00:00:58.803 --> 00:01:06.103
Use a bath on a slice, ionophoresis at a synapse, an injection into one eye, or a

15
00:01:06.103 --> 00:01:09.243
small muscimol injection into one brain area.

16
00:01:10.163 --> 00:01:15.563
Then show the drug works at that dose, by abolishing the response to a known

17
00:01:15.563 --> 00:01:18.423
agonist, or by recording the target's output.

18
00:01:19.267 --> 00:01:25.667
Now measure the function you care about and the baseline side by side, with the drug

19
00:01:25.667 --> 00:01:30.367
on and off. Baseline is the control for general damage.

20
00:01:31.247 --> 00:01:37.147
A drug that blocks the induced change but leaves baseline alone shows the target is

21
00:01:37.147 --> 00:01:41.387
needed for induction under that protocol, not routine transmission.

22
00:01:42.350 --> 00:01:44.630
Here is an idealised example.

23
00:01:45.630 --> 00:01:47.950
Resting heart rate is 70 per minute.

24
00:01:48.730 --> 00:01:50.870
After atropine it is 110.

25
00:01:51.950 --> 00:01:54.070
After propranolol it is 62.

26
00:01:55.110 --> 00:01:56.850
After both it is 100.

27
00:01:57.850 --> 00:02:00.950
So parasympathetic braking dominates at rest.

28
00:02:01.830 --> 00:02:07.090
This holds only if each drug fully blocks only its own division at that dose.

29
00:02:07.983 --> 00:02:10.903
Many drugs hit more than one target.

30
00:02:11.643 --> 00:02:18.363
The authors profiled 65 reportedly specific kinase inhibitors against 70 to 80

31
00:02:18.363 --> 00:02:23.823
kinases. Many were not specific, so the authors urge considerable caution.

32
00:02:24.603 --> 00:02:28.703
So use the best-characterized drug at the lowest dose that works.

33
00:02:29.203 --> 00:02:34.343
Then add controls: a second drug from a different chemical series, and a matched

34
00:02:34.343 --> 00:02:35.403
inactive analogue.

35
00:02:35.963 --> 00:02:38.983
The best control is a drug-resistant target.

36
00:02:39.703 --> 00:02:46.083
One changed cysteine made fission yeast CRM1 resistant to leptomycin B, and export

37
00:02:46.083 --> 00:02:47.383
stayed normal in the drug.

38
00:02:48.183 --> 00:02:55.443
In 1979 Pittman and Oppenheim immobilized chick embryos with curare and three

39
00:02:55.443 --> 00:03:02.563
toxins. With alpha-cobratoxin, about 21,000 lumbar motoneurons survived to day

40
00:03:02.563 --> 00:03:06.923
10, versus 13,500 in untreated controls.

41
00:03:07.823 --> 00:03:12.683
All four blockers were about equally effective, which argued against one drug's side

42
00:03:12.683 --> 00:03:20.823
effect. In 1986 Stryker and Harris injected tetrodotoxin (TTX) into both eyes of

43
00:03:20.823 --> 00:03:26.503
kittens. Ocular dominance columns, eye-specific stripes in cortex, did not form.

44
00:03:27.743 --> 00:03:32.063
Controls for the injection, systemic TTX and visual deprivation did not block them.

45
00:03:32.817 --> 00:03:40.557
In 1983 Collingridge, Kehl and McLennan applied APV by ionophoresis, in doses that

46
00:03:40.557 --> 00:03:43.377
abolished responses to the NMDA agonist.

47
00:03:44.077 --> 00:03:49.197
In rat hippocampal slices, it had no effect on the field synaptic potential, but it

48
00:03:49.197 --> 00:03:51.197
prevented long-term potentiation.

49
00:03:51.837 --> 00:03:57.257
So the NMDA receptor is needed to start lasting change under that protocol, not for

50
00:03:57.257 --> 00:03:58.157
baseline transmission.

51
00:03:58.157 --> 00:04:02.877
Keep this rule. A block shows what is needed here, at this dose.

52
00:04:03.297 --> 00:04:04.857
It does not show what is enough.
